.TH "PVIMG" "1" "2026-08-11" "s390-tools" "Pvimg Manual"
.nh
.ad l
.SH "NAME"
pvimg \- Create and inspect IBM Secure Execution images
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.nf
.fam C
 \fBpvimg\fR [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>
.fam C
.fi
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
Use \fBpvimg\fP to create an IBM Secure Execution image, which can be loaded
using zipl or QEMU. \fBpvimg\fP can also be used to inspect existing Secure
Execution images.
.SH "PVIMG COMMANDS"
\fBpvimg\-create(1)\fR
.RS 4
Create an IBM Secure Execution image.

Create a new IBM Secure Execution image. Only create these images in a trusted
environment, such as your workstation. The \fBpvimg create\fR command creates
randomly generated keys to protect the image. The generated image can then be
booted on an IBM Secure Execution system as a KVM guest.
.RE
.PP
\fBpvimg\-info(1)\fR
.RS 4
Print information about the IBM Secure Execution image.

The \fBinfo\fR subcommand extracts and displays information about an existing
IBM Secure Execution image, including information from the Secure Execution
header. By default, the header is displayed without verifying its integrity and
authenticity. Use \fB\-\-hdr\-key\fR to authenticate and verify the header
integrity.

Output can be formatted as human\-readable text or JSON. Use \fB\-\-format\fR to
control the output format and level of detail.

SECURITY NOTE: Without \fB\-\-hdr\-key\fR, the displayed information is NOT
verified and should not be trusted.
.RE
.PP
\fBpvimg\-test(1)\fR
.RS 4
Test different aspects of an existing IBM Secure Execution image
.RE
.SH "OPTIONS"
\-v, \-\-verbose
.RS 4
Provide more detailed output.
.RE
.PP
\-q, \-\-quiet
.RS 4
Provide less output.
.RE
.PP
\-\-version
.RS 4
Print version information and exit.
.RE
.PP
\-h, \-\-help
.RS 4
Print help (see a summary with \fB\-h\fR).
.RE
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP 8
.B 0 \- Program finished successfully
The command was executed successfully.
.TP 8
.B 1 \- Generic error
Something went wrong during the operation. Refer to the error
message.
.TP 8
.B 2 \- Usage error
The command was used incorrectly, for example: unsupported command
line flag, or wrong number of arguments.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
\fBpvimg\-create\fR(1), \fBpvimg\-info\fR(1), \fBpvimg\-test\fR(1), \fBzipl\fR(8), \fBqemu\fR(1)